{"name":"Hazy Trail","hex":"#dcdace","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hazy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Thick or obscured with haze.","Not clear or transparent."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A variety of beer (typically a pale ale, India pale ale, or double India pale ale) golden in color with softer mouthfeel and sweeter taste than its non-hazy counterpart."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hazy"},{"term":"trail","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To follow behind (someone or something); to tail (someone or something).","To drag (something) behind on the ground."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The track or indication marking the route followed by something that has passed, such as the footprints of animal on land or the contrail of an airplane in the sky.","A route for travel over land, especially a narrow, unpaved pathway for use by hikers, horseback riders, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/trail"}],"entries":[{"title":"Haze","blocks":[{"text":"Haze is traditionally an atmospheric phenomenon in which dust, smoke, and other dry particulates suspended in air obscure visibility and the clarity of the sky. The World Meteorological Organization manual of codes includes a classification of particulates causing horizontal obscuration into categories of fog, ice fog, steam fog, mist, haze, smoke, volcanic ash, dust, sand, and snow. Sources for particles that cause haze include farming, traffic, industry, windy weather, volcanic activity and wildfires.\nSeen from afar and depending on the direction of view with respect to the Sun, haze may appear brownish or bluish, while mist tends to be bluish grey instead. Whereas haze often is considered a phenomenon occurring in dry air, mist formation is a phenomenon in saturated, humid air. However, haze particles may act as condensation nuclei that leads to the subsequent vapor condensation and formation of mist droplets; such forms of haze are known as \"wet haze\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/MosqueinAbuja.jpg/330px-MosqueinAbuja.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haze","wikidata":"Q643546"},{"title":"Trail","blocks":[{"text":"A trail, also known as a path or track, is a dirt road or a small paved road, usually through an undeveloped natural area, that is designed primarily for use by pedestrians, equestrians and non-motorized vehicles, although occasionally some off-roading motor vehicles may also use it. In the United Kingdom and Ireland, footpath or hiking trail is the preferred term. However, it is sometimes applied to highways in North America. In the United States, the term was historically used to describe the westward expansion trails, which are land routes into or through wildness regions of the American West used by explorers and migrants."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Trail_between_two_fields_%28Slovenia%2C_Selo_pri_Mirni%29.jpg/330px-Trail_between_two_fields_%28Slovenia%2C_Selo_pri_Mirni%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trail","wikidata":"Q628179"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Striped_Sun_at_Summer_Solstice_2016.jpg/500px-Striped_Sun_at_Summer_Solstice_2016.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Striped_Sun_at_Summer_Solstice_2016.jpg/1280px-Striped_Sun_at_Summer_Solstice_2016.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Striped_Sun_at_Summer_Solstice_2016.jpg/500px-Striped_Sun_at_Summer_Solstice_2016.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Haze over the Mojave Desert from a brush fire in Santa Barbara, California, seen as the Sun descends on the 2016 June solstice, allows the Sun to be photographed without a filter.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStriped_Sun_at_Summer_Solstice_2016.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Spooky_Summer_Sunset.jpg/500px-Spooky_Summer_Sunset.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Spooky_Summer_Sunset.jpg/1280px-Spooky_Summer_Sunset.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Spooky_Summer_Sunset.jpg/500px-Spooky_Summer_Sunset.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Haze as smoke pollution over the Mojave from fires in the Inland Empire, June 2016, demonstrates the loss of contrast to the Sun, and the landscape in general.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpooky_Summer_Sunset.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Air_pollution3.jpg/500px-Air_pollution3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Air_pollution3.jpg/1280px-Air_pollution3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Air_pollution3.jpg/500px-Air_pollution3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAir_pollution3.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Sun_during_sunset.jpg/500px-Sun_during_sunset.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Sun_during_sunset.jpg/1280px-Sun_during_sunset.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.o